commit 621120a34c092c5920d8f76702d76602643c16a1
Author: Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 21:11:05 2026 -0500

    fix(engine): bind a trust reset to its position, and wire the ceremony
    
    Sol r2's two blocking findings.
    
    S3WP3-L-4 — identity alone was the right kind of binding and the wrong amount
    of it. Identity is a property of the bytes, so bytes can carry it: replaying
    the historical predecessor together with the reset reproduces the recorded
    identity exactly, and sol measured K 8 to 5 with no skip record. `follows`
    becomes (physical line, identity). Copied bytes appended later always land
    later, so a stale position is unavoidable no matter how much context comes with
    them — the two-line replay, a full-suffix replay and a wholesale journal
    concatenation now all fail the same check for the same reason. Identity stays
    in the pair, because position alone would accept a truncation that realigns a
    different entry onto the recorded line.
    
    Physical line, never an index among parsed entries: an index is a judgment, and
    a build that classifies one line differently renumbers every entry after it and
    silently voids every reset bound below.
    
    The position claim buys that with an obligation. Binding to a line means
    reading the tail then appending, and there is no lock to hold across the two —
    the journal is append-only precisely so writers need none. A peer appending in
    that window leaves a legitimate reset carrying a stale position. Safe in
    direction, dishonest only if silent, so trust_reset now reads its own entry
    back against the judgment every reader applies and raises TrustResetNotHonored
    if the ceremony did not take.
    
    S3WP3-L-3 — the previous round's fix was hollow. trust_reset still called
    read_journal; last_entry existed but only fixtures used it, and the test that
    was supposed to pin it built an OLD-EPOCH equivocation, which read_journal
    tolerates anyway. The test pinned the helper, not the caller. The caller is
    wired now, and the regression builds a CURRENT-epoch equivocation — the state
    that actually refuses.
    
    One thing measured rather than assumed: under sol's repro K settles at 10, not
    the 8 the ruling predicted. Rejecting a replayed reset is not the same as
    rejecting the replayed advance beside it, which re-imports an abandoned head by
    the ordinary maximum-ordinal rule. Refusal-side, no silent acceptance reachable
    through it, and pinned in its own test rather than hidden inside the repro's.
    
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpCUT3bF3dukDk5xjDejRM

