commit 639097df94b2fb854724872f4c11557ac29c29dd
Author: Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 29 21:25:26 2026 -0500

    fix(engine): a literal replay cannot count twice
    
    Sol r2's divergence, ruled FIX after my "refusal-side, never a way in" was
    refuted at source. I classified by K's DIRECTION; the axis that matters is K's
    REFERENT. A replayed pre-reset advance raised K to a head that was GENUINE, so
    a store restored from the pre-reset backup presented it exactly, compared
    equal, and the unchanged arm gathers no descent evidence by design — the
    ceremonially abandoned state opened silently. A wrongly-raised K naming a real
    abandoned head is a landing pad, not a wall. Verified before fixing.
    
    "Nothing unsigned can tell a re-assertion from a first assertion" was also
    wrong in the way that matters: BYTES can, for a literal replay, and literal
    replay of existing bytes is the threat model. Anything that modifies bytes is
    forgery — the signed grammar's territory, not this gate's.
    
    So a line byte-identical to an earlier line is skipped as a re-assertion,
    through the existing skip channel. Per-line and type-agnostic: a replayed
    audit would poison K exactly as an advance or a reset does, and per-type rules
    would be a list that grows. Order-agnostic, read-side only, no record shape or
    producer change. Headers are exempt because they carry no claim about any head
    and two of them is a create-race artifact this design chose to survive.
    
    Reconciled rather than left contradicting: the replay tests flip back to K=8
    with two skip records, and the divergence comment and its 10-assertion are
    superseded. The residual test flips into the acceptance-vector test it was
    hiding. Position-binding now gets its own case — a line inserted ahead of a
    reset's predecessor, bytes unique so dedup cannot fire — because its old
    coverage came from replays the gate now catches first.
    
    Two fixtures were also wrong and the gate found them: they modelled a
    post-reset advance as byte-identical to a pre-reset one, which no real store
    produces.
    
    Collision direction is pinned rather than argued from improbability: two
    genuine events colliding drop the later line, so K reads LOW, the store
    presents an advance, and the advance branch pays for a verified walk.
    Verification-side, never acceptance.
    
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JpCUT3bF3dukDk5xjDejRM

